     sshhoowwmmoouunntt — show remote nfs mounts on host

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sshhoowwmmoouunntt [--aa | --dd] [--EE] [--ee] [--11] [--33] [_h_o_s_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sshhoowwmmoouunntt utility shows status information about the NFS server on
     _h_o_s_t.  By default it prints the names of all hosts that have NFS file
     systems mounted on the host.  See _N_F_S_: _N_e_t_w_o_r_k _F_i_l_e _S_y_s_t_e_m _P_r_o_t_o_c_o_l
     _S_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n, RFC 1094, Appendix A, and _N_F_S_: _N_e_t_w_o_r_k _F_i_l_e _S_y_s_t_e_m _V_e_r_s_i_o_n
     _3 _P_r_o_t_o_c_o_l _S_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n, Appendix I, for a detailed description of the
     protocol.

     The following options are available:

     --aa, ----aallll
             List all mount points in the form:
                   _h_o_s_t:_d_i_r_p_a_t_h.

     --dd, ----ddiirreeccttoorriieess
             List directory paths of mount points instead of hosts.

     --EE, ----eexxppoorrttss--ssccrriipptt
             Show the _h_o_s_t's exports list in a script-friendly format.  Client
             addresses and the header are not shown, and special characters
             are escaped.

     --ee, ----eexxppoorrttss
             Show the _h_o_s_t's exports list.

     --11      Use mount protocol Version 1, compatible with legacy servers.

     --33      Ignored for backwards compatibility.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mount(8), mount_nfs(8), mountd(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The sshhoowwmmoouunntt utility first appeared in 4.4BSD.

BBUUGGSS
     The mount daemon running on the server only has an idea of the actual
     mounts, since the NFS server is stateless.  The sshhoowwmmoouunntt utility will
     only display the information as accurately as the mount daemon reports
     it.

